# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mobile LAN-dev environment — loaded by `npm run build:mobile:lan`
# (`vite build --mode mobile`).
#
# This profile exists because of one fact: inside a packaged app, `localhost` is
# the PHONE, not the dev machine. Every host this app is configured with today is
# loopback, so a native build made from those values reaches nothing and every
# screen shows a network error that looks exactly like a backend outage.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS A DEVELOPMENT PROFILE. The bundle that ships is `npm run build:mobile`,
# which uses env/.env.production. Nothing here reaches a release artifact.
#
# ⚠ Modes do not stack. Vite loads `.env`, `.env.mobile` and `.env.mobile.local`
# for `--mode mobile` — and NOT `.env.production` — so this file has to repeat
# every value it needs rather than only the ones that differ.
#
# ⚠ `import.meta.env.DEV` is still FALSE here: Vite sets NODE_ENV=production for
# every `vite build` regardless of `--mode`. So VITE_FORCE_MOBILE_AUTH folds away
# and cannot be set from this file — which is correct, because a native build is
# already on the bearer transport via `isNative`.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── The dev machine's address on the phone's network ─────────────────────────
# This is a TAILSCALE address (100.64.0.0/10, the CGNAT range Tailscale owns),
# not a LAN one, and it is the same address agency-dash's mobile profile uses.
# A tailnet address is stable, survives changing Wi-Fi, and reaches the dev
# machine from a phone on mobile data — so the two no longer have to share a
# router.
#
# Two things still have to be true, and neither is about this address:
#   · the phone is signed into the SAME tailnet as this machine — `tailscale
#     status` on either should list the other;
#   · Windows Defender Firewall allows inbound 8022 on the Tailscale adapter.
#     A correct address that TIMES OUT rather than refusing is that signature.
#
# If the tailnet address ever changes, override it in env/.env.mobile.local
# (git-ignored) rather than editing here. `tailscale ip -4` prints it.

# wi-mall backend — all /api calls go here. Must end in /api.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://100.124.149.1:8022/api

# Base URL where uploaded files are served (defaults to the API origin /uploads).
# VITE_FILE_BASE_URL=

# ─── Storefront ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED on mobile, not optional. Two of this app's routes — /preview/store and
# /preview/product/:id — embed the real landing pages in an iframe, and every
# share / copy link a vendor produces is built from this. Left unset it defaults
# to http://localhost:3000, which on a device is the phone itself: the preview
# iframes go blank and every shared link is unopenable.
#
# NB `npm run dev` in the landing app binds loopback only, so this is unreachable
# from the phone until it is started with a host — `npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0`.
VITE_STOREFRONT_BASE_URL=http://100.124.149.1:3000

# ─── Push notifications ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The VITE_FIREBASE_* block is deliberately absent. Those values drive the *web
# push* provider in src/lib/fcm.ts, which P2.6 switches off on native — a service
# worker inside a WebView registers happily and then receives nothing. Native
# push arrives in Phase 4 (P4.1) and is configured by
# android/app/google-services.json from the MESSAGING Firebase project, not by
# any variable in this file.

# ─── Payments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY, deliberately. Billing is read-only on mobile
# (D2 / Phase 5): plan upgrades and credit top-ups route to the web dashboard, so
# no publishable key is needed — and shipping one would only make an unreachable
# path look reachable. src/lib/stripe.ts injects a CDN <script> at runtime, which
# a packaged app should not do at all.
